WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.360
Monday.com. One of the fastest&nbsp;
growing software companies.&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.520
Now it’s dying.
From a $15 billion market cap, to $3 billion.&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.000
And in less than a year.
This company was everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.320
Advertised on basically every&nbsp;
YouTube video. Every Google search.&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.440
So, what happened?
Lawsuits. Marketing spend larger than revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.840
And a billion-dollar promise, quietly walked back.
But it’s not just Monday.&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:45.760
Something massive is happening in software itself.
The entire industry may never be the same.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.080
In the early 2010s, corporate&nbsp;
work was very different.&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.680
Collaboration, project management, tasks,&nbsp;
everything was sorta all over the place.&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.000
Excel spreadsheets, Gantt charts, email chains&nbsp;
forty replies deep, and weekly meetings.&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.880
There were a few products:&nbsp;
Trello, Asana, Microsoft Project.&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.240
But, it was all a bit technical. It&nbsp;
was mostly just for HR or management.&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.960
Two developers, Eran Zinman and Roy Mann,&nbsp;
were looking for a simple way to track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.640
Eran's coworkers' projects at Wix, which were&nbsp;
rapidly scaling, and having those growth pains.&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.520
To solve this, they created a visual,&nbsp;
no-code software that allowed teams to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.120
build custom workflows for projects.
But, there were two big problems.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.760
It wasn't called Monday. Not yet.
It was called Dapulse.&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.920
A name might not sound like&nbsp;
a big problem… but it was.&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:48.080
And second, when it came time to break into the&nbsp;
Western market, Dapulse faced major obstacles.&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.320
In project management, companies&nbsp;
are rarely quick to switch tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.680
even if their current system isn't great.
Especially with established players like Asana and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.760
Microsoft Project already dominating the space.
And, even when they did get attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.920
the product name worked against them.
In Silicon Valley, Dapulse struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.880
to be taken seriously. Every sales call&nbsp;
focused on the name instead of the product.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.360
For marketing director Joel&nbsp;
Goldstein, it was pretty embarrassing.&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.320
"I knew it was really bad when I went on TV for&nbsp;
the first time in my life and all the anchor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:28.720
could focus on was how bad the name Dapulse was."
They had to change the name. They already had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.600
strong product, but no one was focused on that.
But, to what?

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.920
And that wasn't the only change.
Instead of competing directly with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.720
Asana and Microsoft, which mainly targeted&nbsp;
managers, developers and engineers.&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.200
Monday went wider.
They targeted non-technical teams like marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.920
departments, creative agencies, and HR teams.
And they would go way broader down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:58.000
line. But for now, it was working.
Before the rebrand in November 2017, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.720
company had 18,000 paying customers. By 2018, that&nbsp;
number had grown to 35,000, then 80,000 in 2019.&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.360
And with more users, came more&nbsp;
funding, from $25 million in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.760
to $50 million within a year, and then to $150&nbsp;
million, valuing the company at $1.9 billion.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.880
That name, Monday.com, was suddenly&nbsp;
everywhere. It was universal.&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.840
It sounded synonymous with work itself.
But all of this was nothing.&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.040
Monday's real big break came during the pandemic.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.120
The pandemic ushered in remote work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.800
and companies needed software to better manage&nbsp;
the team and tasks, without being in person.&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.840
A lot of management at the time happened&nbsp;
in person. Meetings, talking at desks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:50.160
whiteboards. A lot of it was in the same room,&nbsp;
so the software didn't need to be as strong.&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.720
But when everyone's remote…&nbsp;
that's all out the window.&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.440
A unified platform like Monday, was&nbsp;
perfect. Everyone was on the same page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.600
even if no one was in the same room.
Sign-ups exploded. Revenue climbed 85%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:12.080
year over year to $58 million that quarter.
But, they were still losing a lot of money.&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.800
Regardless, it was the perfect&nbsp;
time to take the next step: an IPO.&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.760
Zoom and Salesforce had already&nbsp;
poured in $150 million before the IPO.&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.640
With shares priced between $125&nbsp;
and $140, the company raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:35.440
$574 million at a $6.8–7.8 billion valuation.
The stock climbed to $400 per share by late 2021.&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.280
Everything was going great.
But there are big gaps in this story.&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.480
Monday was still losing a ton of money.
Also, how did Monday manage to compete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:52.240
with Asana so quickly, while Trello and&nbsp;
Notion were also rising and taking ground?&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:05:03.120
Well, when we look closer, things&nbsp;
start to get a bit strange.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:10.240
Take a look at their YouTube channel. Over 1&nbsp;
billion views. But most of that is paid for.&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:18.480
Monday was spending a lot on advertising. That&nbsp;
might not sound crazy, but, this was a lot, a lot.&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.360
The amount of money Monday.com was&nbsp;
pouring into advertising, is staggering.&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.080
But for now, Monday was pretty much&nbsp;
doing the opposite of everyone else.&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.320
Remember what I said about them going broad?

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.880
Well, in the words of their AdWords team leader:
[Rotem Shay]: "Our target decision-maker [was]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.480
basically anyone [with] a computer [who] works&nbsp;
with a team. Some might say that we hacked B2B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:47.840
to become B2C, but I say that our marketing&nbsp;
approach has always been H2H—human to human."&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.120
Their competitors were targeting tech, sales,&nbsp;
managers. But Monday was basically targeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:59.440
everyone but those people. To get as much&nbsp;
growth as possible, and as fast as possible.&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.640
And then, rather than targeting tech companies,&nbsp;
they focused on real-world industries like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.240
construction, wedding planning, and even churches.
They uploaded data from their existing customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.760
database into Facebook's lookalike&nbsp;
feature, allowing their ads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.160
reach people with similar profiles.
Then they expanded beyond Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.000
to Google and in particular YouTube ads.
So many, YouTube, Ads.&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.400
While this was working, Monday was slowly turning&nbsp;
into that one annoying ad you could not skip.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.280
While they were targeting everyone,&nbsp;
they were also annoying everyone.&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.680
In 2020, they spent 118% of their&nbsp;
revenue on sales and marketing.&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.720
Not profit, revenue.
$191 million on S&amp;M&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.520
against $161 million in revenue.
In Q1 2021, around their IPO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.200
they spent $63M, or 107% of&nbsp;
revenue, just on advertising.&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.880
They were trying to capture as much market&nbsp;
share as possible, as fast as possible.&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.520
To be clear: Monday had a&nbsp;
non-GAAP gross margin of 88%.&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.680
Essentially, the product itself is&nbsp;
cheap to deliver, and profitable.&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:19.440
But in the big picture, they were losing money.
Some estimate Monday.com had an operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.800
margin of negative 86%, one of the&nbsp;
lowest out of all SaaS products.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.520
Every dollar they made cost them&nbsp;
one dollar and eighty six cents.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.280
Over time, Monday.com was spending 116%&nbsp;
of its revenue on sales and marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.560
one of the highest figures in all of SaaS.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.000
Though, this was never intended&nbsp;
to be a long-term strategy.&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.720
In their S-1, they stated: "As our business&nbsp;
scales through customer expansion and market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.280
awareness of Monday.com, we anticipate that&nbsp;
sales and marketing expenses as a percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:57.920
of total revenue will continue to decline."
The hope was that it would eventually pay off.&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:06.160
So… would it?

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:11.920
Monday is currently struggling, but we're&nbsp;
still growing. We're trying to get to 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:17.040
million subscribers, and if you can click that&nbsp;
subscribe button, we might be able to reach it!&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.120
Setup
In November 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.880
Monday officially passed $1 billion in&nbsp;
annual revenue, with 90% gross margins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.520
$45 million in net income, and&nbsp;
$82.4 million in free cash flow.&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.320
Everything had panned out.&nbsp;
Monday was making profit. Great…&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:40.320
Then something strange happened.&nbsp;
The stock fell 20% in a single day.&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.120
Their market cap dropped by $3 billion overnight.
Why?&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:50.640
Something massive was taking place. People&nbsp;
just didn't realize how big this would be.&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.920
Tension
Wall Street believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:58.560
there were problems ahead, so they lowered the&nbsp;
expected growth rate to 28%, down from 33%.&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:03.840
Eran Zinman, in response, said: "We have&nbsp;
no control over market expectations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:09.520
which have risen significantly in recent months.&nbsp;
From a performance perspective, everything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:15.680
proceeding as planned, but perhaps the market&nbsp;
expected us to beat forecasts even more."&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.840
To be fair, Monday was&nbsp;
performing exceptionally well.&nbsp;

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:26.080
But… Wall Street was right, and we would see&nbsp;
how at the end of the next financial year.&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:33.920
In Q2 2025, they beat revenue expectations,&nbsp;
and reached 299 million, growth of 29%.&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:37.840
But growth slowed among small&nbsp;
businesses, their core market.&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.720
Monday said this was thanks&nbsp;
to Google algorithm changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.960
like the new AI overviews dropping traffic.
But nonetheless, investors were growing concerned.&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.800
So they pushed:

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.160
[Brent Thill, Jefferies LLC]: "I think there&nbsp;
are a lot of questions just as it relates to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.813
the Google change and what percent that impacted&nbsp;
your business if you think about just x, y,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.813 --> 00:10:03.440
z percent covered in this category from Google."
CEO Eran Zinman responded saying:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.760
"I don't exactly, I don't control, we don't&nbsp;
control, exactly, Google will play this out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.800
how the world is going to be. So we're trying&nbsp;
to understand what the implications are."&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:16.528
Then another question:
[Matthew Bullock, Equity Research Analyst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.528 --> 00:10:19.840
Bank of America Merrill Lynch]: "And then&nbsp;
help us understand, why call this out now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:25.985
How did the influence of Google SEO disruption&nbsp;
change this quarter versus 1Q, for example?"&nbsp;

00:10:25.985 --> 00:10:32.640
[Eran Zinman]: So look, I think like we said,&nbsp;
we optimize in real-time. We just budget daily.&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.840
This seems to imply they were not aware&nbsp;
of the problem until they saw Q2 results.&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.160
But a reminder during all this…&nbsp;
Monday had beaten projections.&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:47.200
Only by about 7 million, but still.&nbsp;
Growth was ahead of expectations.&nbsp;

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:52.320
Unfortunately, stock performance is more&nbsp;
about the future than anything else.&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:57.600
So the stock dropped 26%.
Something was happening…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.200
The SaaS world was changing.
For now, Monday had to ease concerns.&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:09.760
So to combat the doubt, in September 2025, on&nbsp;
Analyst and Investors Day they made a big promise.&nbsp;

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:16.400
$1.8 billion in annual revenue by 2027.
Current annual revenue was 1.2 billion.&nbsp;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:21.200
That's a big leap given their&nbsp;
market saturation. And they knew it.&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.960
Because a few months later, they&nbsp;
pulled back these expectations.&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:33.920
From $1.8 billion to about $1.45 billion.&nbsp;
Slower growth of 18–19% versus 27% in 2025.&nbsp;

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.520
Monday said it was due to "choppiness&nbsp;
in the no-touch demand environment",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:43.120
along with longer enterprise sales cycles.
Investors were not happy, sending the stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.640
down 21% in a single day.
When you promise one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:52.720
then change your mind, people get annoyed,&nbsp;
especially if that promise made people invest.&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:57.840
But it was about to get way worse.
Multiple law firms came forward with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.480
class action lawsuits, each alleging that&nbsp;
management hid slowing customer growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:08.400
weaker expansions, and longer sales cycles,&nbsp;
making the $1.8 billion target unrealistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.480
yet executives still publicly&nbsp;
reaffirmed it as conditions worsened.&nbsp;

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.240
"Companies that make specific promises&nbsp;
to investors about future performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:21.520
have an obligation to disclose known risks&nbsp;
to those projections. The gap between what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:26.880
monday.com projected in September and what it&nbsp;
disclosed in February raises important questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:31.680
about what was known and when."
But there's a bigger question.&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:38.160
Why was Monday's growth slowing down?
As it turns out, it wasn't just them. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:46.400
entire industry was going through a metamorphosis.
On February 5, 2026, about $285 billion was wiped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:49.520
from software stocks in just 48 hours.
(Show all the stocks falling).&nbsp;

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:58.080
Salesforce fell over 30%, Workday&nbsp;
dropped 33%, HubSpot 39%, Figma 40%.&nbsp;

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.520
Roughly $2 trillion in market&nbsp;
value over the past year.&nbsp;

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:06.240
And of course, that includes Monday.
⚠️ From a peak of $16 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:12.000
they're now worth less than $3.4 billion.
People are calling it the "SaaSpocalypse".&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.560
Small and medium-sized customers are&nbsp;
hugely pulling back from the market.&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.560
Why?
AI.&nbsp;

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:25.280
Many companies are leaving these tools, and&nbsp;
those that aren't, are paying for fewer seats.&nbsp;

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:29.920
Tools like ChatGPT, Cursor, and&nbsp;
Claude Code let companies build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.400
essentially their own internal tools.
It might not be ready to ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:39.760
but to the company, who cares? It works&nbsp;
as an internal tool for management.&nbsp;

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:43.920
For the first time, Atlassian, the&nbsp;
giant of these enterprise platforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:48.800
reported fewer enterprise seats.
So, what is Monday doing about it?&nbsp;

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:52.080
They're essentially killing&nbsp;
the seat-based business model.&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:57.600
And it is changing from a "work management&nbsp;
platform" to an "AI Work Platform."&nbsp;

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:01.520
A platform for automation,&nbsp;
AI credits and AI agents.&nbsp;

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:06.960
From per-seat pricing to per-credit pricing.
They're also changing their core product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:12.480
boards, the spreadsheet grid for tracking&nbsp;
work, to different agents. Monday Sidekick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:17.440
Magic, Vibe, all about agents.
Might sound annoying, another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:24.160
company pivoting to AI. But for Monday,&nbsp;
their market is speaking pretty clearly.&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:28.320
Their business model no longer works.
They have to change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:33.360
or they'll disappear entirely.
I also do respect Eran Zinman.&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:39.920
He still holds about 80% of his shares since the&nbsp;
IPO, he isn't cashing out and abandoning ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:43.760
he's trying to figure out how&nbsp;
to solve this new problem.&nbsp;

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:49.040
Another company tool struggling is Slack,&nbsp;
but for totally different reasons to Monday.&nbsp;

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:54.560
They used to be dominating, but despite&nbsp;
being the better product, ended up losing.&nbsp;

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.560
